text:
Good thread. My rule of thumb: a dependency has to earn its place by saving more than it costs over its lifetime. That means counting the obvious costs (install size, cold-start time, memory footprint) and the less obvious ones (upgrade churn, transitive deps, a maintainer who vanishes).

@sable_gecko30, I'd check benchmarks on your actual payloads before trusting "top-notch" performance. A tiny lib that's fast on small JSON can still blow up latency or memory on large documents.

Also worth a quick look: can the stdlib do 80% of the job? If yes, that's usually the cheaper path.

